1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Funding for this program is provided by...

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Additional funding provided by...

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
And provided by...

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Last time, we argued about the case

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
of the Queen versus Dudley and Stevens.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The lifeboat case. The case of cannibalism at sea.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
And with the arguments about the lifeboat in mind,

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
the arguments for and against what

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Dudley and Stevens did in mind,

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,500
let's turn back to the philosophy,

11
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:00,000
the utilitarian philosophy of Jeremy Bentham.

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Bentham was born in England in 1748.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
At the age of 12, he went to Oxford.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,000
At 15, he went to London.

15
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
At 16, he went to law school.

16
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,500
He was admitted to the bar at

17
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,000
age 19, but he never practiced law.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,500
Instead, he devoted his life

19
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,000
to jurisprudence and moral philosophy.

20
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Last time, we began to

21
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
consider Bentham's version of utilitarianism.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The main idea is simply stated, and it's this.

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
The highest principle of morality,

24
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
whether personal or political morality,

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,500
is to maximize the general

26
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:46,000
welfare, or the collective happiness,

27
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
or the overall balance of pleasure over pain.

28
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
In a phrase, maximize utility.

29
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Bentham arrives at this principle

30
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
by the following line of reasoning.

31
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
We're all governed by pain and pleasure.

32
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
They are our sovereign masters.

33
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,500
And so any moral system

34
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:08,000
has to take account of them.

35
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
How best to take account?

36
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
By maximizing.

37
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
And this leads to the principle of

38
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
the greatest good for the greatest number.

39
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
What exactly should we maximize?

40
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Bentham tells us, happiness, or more precisely, utility.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Maximizing utility is a principle not only for individuals,

42
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
but also for communities and for legislators.

43
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
What, after all, is a community?

44
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Bentham asks.

45
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
It's the sum of the individuals who comprise it.

46
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
And that's why, in deciding the best policy,

47
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
in deciding what the law should be,

48
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
in deciding what's just,

49
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
citizens and legislators should ask themselves the question,

50
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,500
if we add up all

51
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:01,000
of the benefits of this policy,

52
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and so on,

53
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
and subtract all of the costs,

54
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:13,000
the right thing to do is the one that maximizes

55
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
the balance of happiness over suffering.

56
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
That's what it means to maximize utility.

57
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Now, today, I want to see

58
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
whether you agree or disagree with it.

59
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
And it often goes, this utilitarian logic,

60
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
under the name of cost-benefit analysis,

61
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,500
which is used by companies

62
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:44,000
and by governments all the time.

63
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
And what it involves is placing a value,

64
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
usually a dollar value, to stand for utility

65
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
on the costs and the benefits of various proposals.

66
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Recently, in the Czech Republic,

67
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
there was a proposal to

68
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
increase the excise tax on smoking.

69
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Philip Morris, the tobacco company,

70
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
does huge business in the Czech Republic.

71
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
They commissioned a study, a cost-benefit analysis,

72
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
of smoking in the Czech Republic.

73
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
And what their cost-benefit analysis found

74
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,000
was the government gains by having Czech citizens smoke.

75
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Now, how do they gain?

76
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It's true that there are negative effects

77
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
to the public finance of the Czech government,

78
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
because there are increased healthcare costs

79
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
for people who develop smoking-related diseases.

80
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
On the other hand, there were positive effects,

81
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,500
and those were added up on

82
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:55,000
the other side of the ledger.

83
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
The positive effects included, for the most part,

84
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
the various tax revenues that the government derives

85
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
from the sale of cigarette products,

86
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
but it also included healthcare savings to the government

87
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
when people die early,

88
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
pension savings, you don't have

89
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
to pay pensions for as long,

90
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and also savings in housing costs for the elderly.

91
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
And when all of the

92
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
costs and benefits were added up,

93
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
the Philip Morris study found

94
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
that there is a net public finance gain

95
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,000
in the Czech Republic of $147 million.

96
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,500
And given the savings in

97
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:41,000
housing and healthcare and pension costs,

98
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
the government enjoys the savings of over $1,200

99
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
for each person who dies prematurely due to smoking.

100
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Cost-benefit analysis.

101
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Now,

102
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
those among you who are defenders of utilitarianism

103
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
may think that this is an unfair test.

104
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Philip Morris was pilloried in the press

105
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
and they issued an apology for this heartless calculation.

106
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
You may say that what's missing here

107
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
is something that the utilitarian can easily incorporate,

108
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
namely,

109
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
the value to the person and to the families

110
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
of those who die from lung cancer.

111
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
What about the value of life?

112
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Some cost-benefit analyses incorporate

113
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
a measure for the value of life.

114
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
One of the most famous of these

115
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
involved the Ford Pinto case.

116
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Did any of you read about that?

117
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
This was back in the 1970s.

118
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Do you remember what the Ford Pinto was,

119
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
that kind of car? Anybody?

120
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,000
It was a small car, a subcompact car, very popular.

121
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
But it had one problem,

122
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:57,500
which is the fuel tank was

123
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,000
at the back of the car,

124
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
and in rear collisions the fuel tank exploded.

125
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
And some people were killed,

126
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
and some severely injured.

127
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Victims of these injuries took Ford to court to sue.

128
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
And in the court case it turned out

129
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
that Ford had long been a fan of the car,

130
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
and that Ford had been a fan of the car

131
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,000
since known about the vulnerable fuel tank,

132
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
and had done a cost-benefit analysis

133
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
to determine whether it would be worth it

134
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
to put in a special shield

135
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
that would protect the fuel tank

136
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and prevent it from exploding.

137
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
They did a cost-benefit analysis.

138
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
The cost per part

139
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
to increase the safety of the Pinto,

140
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:53,000
they calculated at $11 per part.

141
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
And here's,

142
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
this was the cost-benefit analysis

143
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:02,000
that emerged in the trial.

144
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
$11 per part

145
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,000
at 12.5 million cars and trucks

146
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
came to a total cost of $137 million

147
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
to improve the safety.

148
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
But then they calculated the benefits

149
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
of spending all this money on a safer car.

150
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And they counted 180 deaths,

151
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
and they assigned a dollar value,

152
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
$200,000 per death.

153
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,000
180 injuries,

154
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
And then the cost to repair,

155
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
the replacement cost for 2,000 vehicles

156
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
that would be destroyed without the safety device,

157
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,000
$700 per vehicle.

158
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
So the benefits

159
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
turned out to be only

160
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
$49.5 million.

161
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,000
And so they didn't install the device.

162
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Needless to say,

163
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
when this memo

164
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
of the Ford Motor Company's

165
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
cost-benefit analysis came out in the trial,

166
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
it appalled the jurors

167
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,000
who awarded a huge settlement.

168
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Is this a counterexample

169
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
to the utilitarian idea of calculating?

170
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Ford included a measure of the value of life.

171
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Now, who here wants to defend

172
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
cost-benefit analysis

173
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
from this apparent counterexample?

174
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Who has a defense?

175
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Or do you think this completely destroys

176
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:45,000
the whole utilitarian calculus?

177
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Yes.

178
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Well, I think that, once again,

179
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
they've made the same mistake

180
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
that they did,

181
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
that they assigned a dollar value to human life.

182
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,000
And once again,

183
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
they failed to take account things like

184
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
suffering and emotional losses by the families.

185
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
I mean, families lost earnings,

186
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
but they also lost a loved one.

187
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
And that is more valued than $200,000.

188
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Right.

189
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
And wait, wait, wait.

190
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
That's good.

191
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
What's your name?

192
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Julia Roto.

193
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,000
So if 200,000, Julie,

194
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
is too low a figure,

195
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
because it doesn't include the loss of a loved one

196
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
and the loss of those years of life,

197
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
what would be, what do you think,

198
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,000
would be a more accurate number?

199
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
I don't believe I could give a number.

200
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
I think that this sort of analysis

201
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
shouldn't be applied to issues of human life.

202
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
I think it can't be used monetarily.

203
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
So they didn't just put too low a number,

204
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Julie says.

205
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
They were wrong to try

206
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
to put any number at all.

207
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
All right, let's hear someone who...

208
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
You have to adjust your position.

209
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
You have to adjust for inflation.

210
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,000
All right.

211
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Fair enough.

212
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
So what would the number be now?

213
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:07,000
This was 30, this was 35 years ago.

214
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
$2 million.

215
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:11,000
You would put $2 million.

216
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
And what's your name?

217
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Wojtek says we have to allow for inflation.

218
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:18,000
We should be more generous.

219
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
We should be more generous.

220
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Then would you be satisfied

221
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,500
that this is the right

222
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:25,000
way of thinking about the question?

223
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
I guess, unfortunately, it is.

224
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,000
For there needs to be a number put somewhere.

225
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Like, I'm not sure what that number would be,

226
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
but I do agree that there could possibly be

227
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
a number put on human life.

228
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:41,000
All right.

229
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
So Wojtek says,

230
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
and here he disagrees with Julie.

231
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Julie says we can't put a number on human life.

232
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
For the purpose of a cost-benefit analysis.

233
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Wojtek says we have to,

234
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:59,000
because we have to make decisions somehow.

235
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
What do other people think about this?

236
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
Is there anyone prepared

237
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
to defend cost-benefit analysis here?

238
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
As accurate, as desirable?

239
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Yes?

240
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Go ahead.

241
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
I think that if Ford and other car companies

242
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
didn't use cost-benefit analysis,

243
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
they'd eventually go out of business

244
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
because they wouldn't be able to be profitable.

245
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,500
And millions of people wouldn't

246
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:20,000
be able to use their cars

247
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
to get to jobs, to put food on the table,

248
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
to feed their children.

249
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
So I think that if cost-benefit analysis isn't employed,

250
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:32,000
the greater good is sacrificed in this case.

251
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:33,000
All right.

252
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:34,000
Let me ask, what's your name?

253
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Raul.

254
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Raul.

255
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
There was recently a study done about cell phone use

256
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
by a driver when people are driving a car.

257
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
And there's a debate whether that should be banned.

258
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Yeah.

259
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:54,000
And the figure was that some 2,000 people die

260
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:00,000
as a result of accidents each year using cell phones.

261
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
And yet, the cost-benefit analysis,

262
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
which was done by the

263
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
Center for Risk Analysis at Harvard,

264
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,500
found that if you look at

265
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:12,000
the benefits of the cell phone use

266
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
and you put some value on the life,

267
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
it comes out about the same.

268
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Because of the enormous economic benefit

269
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
of enabling people to take advantage of their time,

270
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
not waste time, be able to make deals

271
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,000
and talk to friends and so on while they're driving.

272
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
Doesn't that suggest that it's a mistake

273
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
to try to put monetary

274
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
figures on questions of human life?

275
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Well, I think that if the great majority of people

276
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
try to derive maximum utility out of a service

277
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
like using cell phones,

278
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
and the convenience that cell phones provide,

279
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,000
that sacrifice is necessary for satisfaction to occur.

280
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
You're an outright utilitarian.

281
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Yes. Okay.

282
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
All right. Then one last question, Raul.

283
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Okay.

284
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
And I put this to Wojtek.

285
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
What dollar figure should be put on human life

286
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
to decide whether to ban the use of cell phones?

287
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Well, I don't want to arbitrarily

288
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
calculate a figure.

289
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
I mean, right now, I think that...

290
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
You want to take it under advisement?

291
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
Yeah, I'll take it under advisement.

292
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
But what, roughly speaking, would it be?

293
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
You've got 2,300 deaths.

294
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
You've got to assign a dollar value

295
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
to know whether you want to prevent those deaths

296
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
by banning the use of cell phones in cars.

297
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Okay.

298
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
So what would your hunch be?

299
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:39,000
How much?

300
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
A million? Two million?

301
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Two million was Wojtek's figure.

302
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:44,000
Yeah.

303
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,000
Is that about right?

304
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Maybe a million.

305
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,000
A million?

306
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Yeah.

307
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,000
You know the...

308
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:52,000
That's good. Thank you.

309
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Okay.

310
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
So these are some of the controversies

311
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
that arise these days from cost-benefit analysis,

312
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:03,000
especially those that involve placing a dollar value

313
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
on everything to be added up.

314
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
Well, now I want to turn to your objections.

315
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
To your objections not necessarily

316
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
to cost-benefit analysis,

317
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
but to the cost-benefit analysis specifically,

318
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
because that's just one version

319
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:22,000
of the utilitarian logic in practice today,

320
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
but to the theory as a whole,

321
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:30,000
to the idea that the right thing to do,

322
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
the just basis for policy and law,

323
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:40,000
is to maximize utility.

324
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
How many disagree with the utilitarian...

325
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:48,000
approach to law and to the common good?

326
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,000
How many agree with it?

327
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
So more agree than disagree.

328
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
So let's hear from the critics.

329
00:15:58,000 --> 00:15:59,000
Yes.

330
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
My main issue with it is that I feel like

331
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:06,000
you can't say that just because someone's in the minority,

332
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
what they want and need is less valuable

333
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
than someone who's in the majority.

334
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
So I guess I have an issue with the idea

335
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
that the greatest good for the greatest number is okay,

336
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
because there's still...

337
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
What about the people who are in the lesser number?

338
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:22,000
Like, it's not fair to them.

339
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:23,500
They didn't have any say

340
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:25,000
in where they wanted to be.

341
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
All right. That's an interesting objection.

342
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,000
You're worried about the effect on the minority.

343
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
Yes.

344
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:33,000
What's your name, by the way?

345
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Anna.

346
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
Who has an answer to Anna's worry

347
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
about the effect on the minority?

348
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:40,000
What do you say to Anna?

349
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
She said that the minorities value less.

350
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
I don't think that's the case,

351
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
because individually the minority's value

352
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
is just the same as the individual of the majority.

353
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:54,000
It's just that the numbers outweigh the minority.

354
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
And, I mean, at a certain

355
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
point, you have to make a decision,

356
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
and I'm sorry for the minority,

357
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:04,000
but sometimes it's for the general, for the greater good.

358
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
For the greater good. Anna, what do you say?

359
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:07,000
What's your name?

360
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,000
Youngda.

361
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:09,000
What do you say to Youngda?

362
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:10,000
Youngda.

363
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
Youngda says you just have to add up people's preferences,

364
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
and those in the minority do have their preferences weighed.

365
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:18,500
Can you give an example of

366
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:20,000
the kind of thing you're worried about

367
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
when you say you're worried about utilitarianism

368
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
violating the concern or respect due the minority?

369
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Okay.

370
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Can you give an example?

371
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:30,500
So, well, with any of

372
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:32,000
the cases that we've talked about,

373
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
like for the shipwreck one,

374
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,000
I think the boy who was eaten still had

375
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,500
as much of a right

376
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:42,000
to live as the other people,

377
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:49,000
and just because he was the minority in that case,

378
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
the one who maybe had less

379
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
of a chance to keep living,

380
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,500
that doesn't mean that the others

381
00:17:55,500 --> 00:17:58,000
automatically have a right to eat him

382
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,500
just because it would give a greater

383
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:03,000
amount of people a chance to live.

384
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,500
So there may be certain

385
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:08,000
rights that the minority members have,

386
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
that the individual has,

387
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:15,000
that shouldn't be traded off for the sake of utility.

388
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:18,000
Yes, Anna?

389
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:21,000
Youngda, this would be a test for you.

390
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Back in ancient Rome,

391
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,500
they threw Christians to the

392
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:29,000
lions in the Colosseum for sport.

393
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:33,000
If you think how the utilitarian calculus would go,

394
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,500
yes, the Christian thrown to

395
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:38,000
the lions suffers enormous, excruciating pain.

396
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,000
But look at the collective ecstasy of the Romans.

397
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Youngda?

398
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:52,000
Well, in that time,

399
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:56,000
in modern day of time,

400
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,500
to give a number to the

401
00:18:58,500 --> 00:19:01,000
happiness given to the people watching,

402
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:07,000
I don't think any policy maker would say

403
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:11,000
the suffering of one person is much, much,

404
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
in comparison to the happiness gained.

405
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
No, but you have to admit

406
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
that if there were enough

407
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
Romans delirious enough with happiness,

408
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
it would outweigh even the most excruciating pain

409
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
of a handful of Christians thrown to the lion.

410
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,500
So we really have here

411
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:33,000
two different objections to utilitarianism.

412
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,000
One has to do with whether utilitarianism,

413
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:42,000
adequately respects individual rights or minority rights.

414
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,500
And the other has to do

415
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:49,000
with the whole idea of aggregating utility

416
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:53,000
or preferences or values.

417
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,500
Is it possible to aggregate all

418
00:19:56,500 --> 00:20:00,000
values to translate them into dollar terms?

419
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:04,000
There was, in the 1930s,

420
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:05,000
a very important argument.

421
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:09,000
And a psychologist who tried

422
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:13,000
to address this second question,

423
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:18,000
he tried to prove what utilitarianism assumes,

424
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:24,000
that it is possible to translate all goods, all values,

425
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:28,000
all human concerns into a single uniform measure.

426
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:31,000
And he did this by conducting

427
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:34,000
a survey of young recipients of relief.

428
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:37,000
This was in the 1930s.

429
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
And he asked them,

430
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
he gave them a list of unpleasant experiences.

431
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:43,000
And he asked them,

432
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
how much would you have to

433
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
be paid to undergo the following experiences?

434
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:50,000
And he kept track.

435
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:51,000
For example,

436
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
how much would you have to be paid

437
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:57,000
to have one upper front tooth pulled out?

438
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,000
Or how much would you have to be

439
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:03,000
paid to have one little toe cut off?

440
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:10,000
Or to eat a live earthworm six inches long?

441
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
Or to live the rest of

442
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
your life on a farm in Kansas?

443
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,500
Or to choke a stray cat

444
00:21:21,500 --> 00:21:24,000
to death with your bare hands?

445
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,500
Now what do you suppose was

446
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:29,000
the most expensive item on that list?

447
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,000
Kansas?

448
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:41,000
You're right, it was Kansas.

449
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:50,000
For Kansas, people said they'd have to be paid $300,000.

450
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:56,000
What do you think,

451
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:57,000
what do you think,

452
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:58,000
what do you think,

453
00:21:58,000 --> 00:21:59,000
what do you think,

454
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
what do you think was the next most expensive?

455
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Not the cat.

456
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,000
Not the tooth.

457
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:10,000
Not the toe.

458
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
The worm.

459
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:19,000
People said you'd have to pay

460
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:22,000
them $100,000 to eat the worm.

461
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:26,000
What do you think was the least expensive item?

462
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
Not the cat.

463
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
Not the cat.

464
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:31,000
The tooth.

465
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:32,000
The tooth.

466
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,500
During the Depression, people were willing to

467
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:39,000
have their tooth pulled for only $4,500.

468
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:48,000
Now, here's what Thorndike concluded from his study.

469
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:51,500
Any want or satisfaction which exists, exists

470
00:22:51,500 --> 00:22:55,000
in some amount and is therefore measurable.

471
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:59,500
The life of a dog or a cat or

472
00:22:59,500 --> 00:23:04,000
a chicken consists of appetites, cravings, desires, and their gratifications.

473
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,000
So does the life of human beings,

474
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:12,000
though the appetites and desires are more complicated.

475
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,000
But what about Thorndike's study?

476
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:24,000
Does it support Bentham's idea that all goods, all values,

477
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:28,000
are captured according to a single uniform measure of value?

478
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,500
Or does the preposterous character of

479
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:33,000
those different items on the list

480
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:38,000
suggest the opposite conclusion that maybe,

481
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:44,000
whether we're talking about life or Kansas or the worm,

482
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:49,000
maybe the things we value and cherish can't be

483
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:54,000
captured according to a single uniform measure of value.

484
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:58,000
And if they can't, what are the

485
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:02,000
consequences for the utilitarian theory of morality?

486
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:05,000
That's a question we'll continue with next time.

487
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
All right, now let's take

488
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
the other part of the poll.

489
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:23,000
Which is the highest experience or pleasure?

490
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,000
How many say Shakespeare?

491
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
How many say Fear Factor?

492
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
No, you can't be serious. Really?

493
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:52,000
Last time, last time we began to consider some objections.

494
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:58,000
To Jeremy Bentham's version of utilitarianism.

495
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:06,000
People raised two objections in the discussion we had.

496
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:12,000
The first was the objection, the claim,

497
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:15,500
that utilitarianism, by concerning itself with

498
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:19,000
the greatest good for the greatest number,

499
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:22,000
fails adequately to respect individual values.

500
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:31,000
Today we have debates about torture and terrorism.

501
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:34,500
Suppose a suspected terrorist

502
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:38,000
was apprehended on September 10th.

503
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:44,500
And you had reason to believe that the suspect had crucial

504
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:51,000
information about an impending terrorist attack that would kill over 3,000 people.

505
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:55,000
And you couldn't extract the information.

506
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:58,500
Would it be just to torture

507
00:25:58,500 --> 00:26:02,000
the suspect to get the information?

508
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:07,000
Or do you say no, there is a

509
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:12,000
categorical moral duty of respect for individual rights?

510
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:15,500
In a way, we're back to the questions

511
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:19,000
we started with about trolley cars and organ transplants.

512
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:20,000
So that's the first issue.

513
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:24,000
And you remember we considered

514
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:28,000
some examples of cost-benefit analysis.

515
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:33,000
But a lot of people were unhappy with cost-benefit analysis

516
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
when it came to placing

517
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
a dollar value on human life.

518
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:43,000
And so that led us to the second objection.

519
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:48,000
It questioned whether it's possible to translate all values

520
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:52,000
into a single uniform measure of value.

521
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,500
It asks, in other words,

522
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:57,000
whether all values are commensurable.

523
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:02,000
Let me give you one other example of an experience.

524
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
This actually is a true story.

525
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:07,000
It comes from personal experience.

526
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:11,500
That raises a question, at least, about whether all

527
00:27:11,500 --> 00:27:16,000
values can be translated without loss into utilitarian terms.

528
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:21,500
Some years ago, when I was a

529
00:27:21,500 --> 00:27:25,000
graduate student, I was at Oxford in England,

530
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:28,000
and they had men's and women's colleges.

531
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
They weren't yet mixed.

532
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:33,500
And the women's colleges had

533
00:27:33,500 --> 00:27:37,000
rules against overnight male guests.

534
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:40,500
By the 1970s, these rules

535
00:27:40,500 --> 00:27:44,000
were rarely enforced and easily violated.

536
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
Or so I was told.

537
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:51,500
By the late 1970s, when I was

538
00:27:51,500 --> 00:27:55,000
there, pressure grew to relax these rules,

539
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:58,000
and it became the subject of debate

540
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:01,000
among the faculty at St. Anne's College,

541
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:03,000
which was one of these all-women's colleges.

542
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:07,000
The older women on the faculty were traditionalists.

543
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:12,000
They were opposed to change on conventional moral grounds.

544
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:15,000
But times had changed, and they were embarrassed

545
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
to give the true groundwork.

546
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,500
The men's colleges were not the only ones

547
00:28:20,500 --> 00:28:23,000
to change on conventional moral grounds for their objection.

548
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:25,500
And so they translated

549
00:28:25,500 --> 00:28:28,000
their arguments into utilitarian terms.

550
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:31,000
If men stay overnight, they argued

551
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:34,000
the costs to the college will increase.

552
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
How, you might wonder?

553
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:39,000
Well, they'll want to take baths, and

554
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:42,000
that'll use up hot water, they said.

555
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,500
Furthermore, they argued, we'll have

556
00:28:44,500 --> 00:28:47,000
to replace the mattresses more often.

557
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
They met these arguments

558
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
by adopting the following compromise.

559
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:55,000
Each woman could have a maximum

560
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,000
of three overnight male guests each week.

561
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,000
They didn't say whether it had to

562
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
be the same one or three different.

563
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:08,000
Provided, and this was the compromise,

564
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:11,500
provided the guest paid 50 pence

565
00:29:11,500 --> 00:29:15,000
to defray the cost to the college.

566
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,000
The next day,

567
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:21,000
the national headline in the national newspaper read,

568
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:29,000
St. Anne's girls, 50 pence a night.

569
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:32,500
Another illustration of the

570
00:29:32,500 --> 00:29:36,000
difficulty of translating all values,

571
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:39,000
in this case a certain idea of virtue,

572
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:42,000
into utilitarian terms.

573
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:46,000
So, that's all to illustrate

574
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:50,000
the second objection to utilitarianism,

575
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:52,000
at least the part of that objection

576
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:57,000
that questions whether utilitarianism is right

577
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:02,000
to assume that we can assume the uniformity of value,

578
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:05,000
the commensurability of all values,

579
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,000
and translate all moral

580
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:11,000
considerations into dollars or money.

581
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:15,000
But there is a second aspect to this worry

582
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:19,000
about aggregating values and preferences.

583
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:26,000
Why should we weigh all preferences that people have

584
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:29,000
without assessing whether they're

585
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:32,000
good preferences or bad preferences?

586
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:36,000
Shouldn't we distinguish between

587
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:40,000
higher pleasures and lower pleasures?

588
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:45,000
Now, part of the appeal of not making any claims

589
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:47,000
about qualitative distinctions,

590
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,000
about the worth of people's preferences,

591
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:52,000
part of the appeal is

592
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:55,000
that it is nonjudgmental and egalitarian.

593
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:58,000
The Benthamite utilitarian says,

594
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
everybody's preferences count,

595
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:04,000
and they count regardless of what people want,

596
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:08,000
regardless of what makes different people happy.

597
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:13,000
For Bentham, all that matters, you'll remember,

598
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:15,500
are the intensity and the

599
00:31:15,500 --> 00:31:18,000
duration of a pleasure or pain.

600
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:21,000
The so-called higher pleasures or nobler virtues

601
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:24,000
are simply those, according to Bentham,

602
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:29,000
that produce stronger, longer pleasure.

603
00:31:29,000 --> 00:31:32,000
He had a famous phrase to express this idea.

604
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:35,000
The quantity of pleasure being equal,

605
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:39,000
pushpin is as good as poetry.

606
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:41,000
What was pushpin?

607
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:43,000
It was some kind of a child's game,

608
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:44,000
like tiddlywinks.

609
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:48,000
Pushpin is as good as poetry, Bentham says.

610
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:51,000
And lying behind this idea, I think,

611
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:54,000
is the claim, the intuition,

612
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:57,000
that it's a presumption to judge

613
00:31:57,000 --> 00:32:00,000
whose pleasures are intrinsically

614
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:03,000
higher or worthier or better.

615
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:08,000
And there is something attractive in this refusal to judge.

616
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:12,000
After all, some people like Mozart, others, Madonna.

617
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:17,000
Some people like ballet, others, bowling.

618
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:20,000
Who's to say, Bentham might argue,

619
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:23,000
who's to say which of these pleasures,

620
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:30,000
whose pleasures, are higher, worthier, nobler than others?

621
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:36,000
But is that right?

622
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:40,000
This refusal to make qualitative distinctions.

623
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:45,000
Can we altogether dispense with the idea

624
00:32:45,000 --> 00:32:47,000
that certain things we take pleasure in

625
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:53,000
are better or worthier than others?

626
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:54,500
Think back to the case

627
00:32:54,500 --> 00:32:56,000
of the Romans and the Colosseum.

628
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:59,000
One thing that troubled people about that practice

629
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:01,500
is that it seemed to

630
00:33:01,500 --> 00:33:04,000
violate the rights of the Christian.

631
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:07,000
Another way of objecting to what's going on there

632
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:10,000
is that the pleasure that the Romans take

633
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:13,000
in this bloody spectacle,

634
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:19,000
should that pleasure, which is a base kind of corrupt,

635
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,000
degrading pleasure,

636
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:25,000
should that even be valorized or weighed

637
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:34,000
in deciding what the general welfare is?

638
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:39,000
So here are the objections to Bentham's utilitarianism.

639
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:43,000
And now we turn to someone who tried to respond

640
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:45,000
to those objections,

641
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:50,000
a later-day utilitarian, John Stuart Mill.

642
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:54,000
So what we need to examine now

643
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:59,000
is whether John Stuart Mill had a convincing reply

644
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:05,000
to these objections to utilitarianism.

645
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:08,000
John Stuart Mill was born in 1806.

646
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:13,000
His father, James Mill, was a disciple of Bentham's.

647
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:17,000
And James Mill set about giving his son,

648
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:20,000
John Stuart Mill, a model education.

649
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:23,000
He was a child prodigy, John Stuart Mill.

650
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:25,000
He knew Latin at the age of, sorry,

651
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:27,000
Greek at the age of three, Latin at eight,

652
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:33,000
and age 10, he wrote a history of Roman law.

653
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:37,000
At age 20, he had a nervous breakdown.

654
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:43,000
This left him in a depression for five years.

655
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:44,500
But at age 25, what helped

656
00:34:44,500 --> 00:34:46,000
lift him out of this depression

657
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:49,000
is that he met Harriet Taylor.

658
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
She and Mill got married.

659
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,000
They lived happily ever after.

660
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:56,000
And it was under her influence

661
00:34:56,000 --> 00:35:01,000
that John Stuart Mill tried to humanize utilitarianism.

662
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:04,000
What Mill tried to do was to see

663
00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:08,000
whether the utilitarian calculus could be enlarged

664
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:14,000
and modified to accommodate humanitarian concerns

665
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:19,000
like the concern to respect individual rights

666
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:22,000
and also to address the distinction

667
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:25,000
between higher and lower pleasures.

668
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:30,000
In 1859, Mill wrote a famous book on liberty,

669
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:32,000
the main point of which was the importance

670
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:35,000
of defending individual rights and minority rights.

671
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:37,000
And in 1861,

672
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:40,000
toward the end of his life,

673
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:41,500
he wrote the book we

674
00:35:41,500 --> 00:35:43,000
read as part of this course,

675
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:45,000
Utilitarianism.

676
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:47,000
He makes it clear that utility

677
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:49,000
is the only standard of morality,

678
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:51,000
in his view.

679
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:54,000
So he's not challenging Bentham's premise.

680
00:35:54,000 --> 00:35:56,000
He's affirming it.

681
00:35:56,000 --> 00:35:57,000
He says very explicitly,

682
00:35:57,000 --> 00:36:00,000
the sole evidence it is possible to produce

683
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:02,000
that anything is desirable

684
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:06,000
is that people actually do desire it.

685
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:08,000
So he stays with the idea

686
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:11,000
that de facto, actual, empirical desires

687
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:15,000
are the only basis for moral judgment.

688
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:18,000
But then, on page eight,

689
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:20,000
also in chapter two,

690
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:23,000
he argues that it is possible for a utilitarian

691
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:27,000
to distinguish higher from lower pleasures.

692
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:32,000
Now, those of you who have read Mill already,

693
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,000
how, according to him,

694
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:36,000
is it possible to draw that distinction?

695
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:39,000
How can a utilitarian

696
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:42,000
distinguish qualitatively higher pleasures

697
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:47,000
from lesser ones, base ones, unworthy ones?

698
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:48,000
Yes?

699
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:50,000
If you've tried both of them,

700
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:54,000
and you prefer the higher one naturally, always.

701
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:56,000
That's great. That's right.

702
00:36:56,000 --> 00:36:57,000
What's your name?

703
00:36:57,000 --> 00:36:58,000
John.

704
00:36:58,000 --> 00:37:01,000
So, as John points out,

705
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:04,000
Mill says, here's the test.

706
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:06,000
Since we can't step outside

707
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:10,000
actual desires, actual preferences,

708
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:13,000
that would violate utilitarian premises.

709
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:16,000
The only test

710
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:20,000
of whether a pleasure is higher or lower

711
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:24,000
is whether someone who has experienced both

712
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:27,000
would prefer it.

713
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:31,000
And here, in chapter two,

714
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:33,000
we see the passage where Mill makes the point

715
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:35,000
that John just described.

716
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:39,000
Of two pleasures, if there be one to which all,

717
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:43,000
or almost all, who have experience of both,

718
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:45,000
give a decided preference,

719
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:47,000
irrespective of any feeling of

720
00:37:47,000 --> 00:37:49,000
moral obligation to prefer it,

721
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:52,000
in other words, no outside, no independent standard,

722
00:37:52,000 --> 00:37:57,000
then that is the more desirable pleasure.

723
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:59,000
What do people think about that argument?

724
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:01,000
Does that...

725
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:03,000
Does it succeed?

726
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:05,000
How many think that it does succeed?

727
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:08,000
Of arguing within utilitarian terms

728
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:11,000
for a distinction between higher and lower pleasures?

729
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:17,000
How many think it doesn't succeed?

730
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:20,000
I want to hear your reasons.

731
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:23,000
But before we give the reasons,

732
00:38:23,000 --> 00:38:26,000
let's do an experiment

733
00:38:26,000 --> 00:38:31,000
of Mill's claim.

734
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:33,000
In order to do this experiment,

735
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:41,000
we're going to look at three short excerpts

736
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:44,000
of popular entertainment.

737
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:48,000
The first one is a Hamlet soliloquy.

738
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:53,000
It'll be followed by two other experiences.

739
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:57,000
See what you think.

740
00:38:57,000 --> 00:39:00,000
What a piece of work is a man.

741
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:02,000
How noble in reason.

742
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:04,000
How infinite in faculties.

743
00:39:04,000 --> 00:39:05,000
In form and moving.

744
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:07,000
How express and admirable.

745
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:09,000
In action, how like an angel.

746
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:11,000
In apprehension, how like a god.

747
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:13,000
The beauty of the world.

748
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:15,000
The paragon of animals.

749
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:17,000
And yet to me,

750
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:20,000
what is this quintessence of dust?

751
00:39:20,000 --> 00:39:22,000
Man delights not me.

752
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:42,000
Imagine a world

753
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:45,000
where your greatest fears become reality.

754
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:47,000
Ow, they're biting me!

755
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:49,000
Each show, six contestants from around the country

756
00:39:49,000 --> 00:39:52,000
battle each other in three extreme stunts.

757
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:54,000
These stunts are designed to challenge the contestants

758
00:39:54,000 --> 00:39:58,000
both physically and mentally.

759
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:01,000
Six contestants, three stunts,

760
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:03,000
one winner.

761
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:04,000
Yes!

762
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Fear Factor.

763
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:18,000
Hi, diddly-ho, pedal to the metalophiles.

764
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:21,000
Landers, since when do you like anything cool?

765
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:23,000
Well, I don't care for the speed,

766
00:40:23,000 --> 00:40:25,000
but I can't get enough of that safety gear.

767
00:40:25,000 --> 00:40:27,000
Helmets, roll bars, caution flags.

768
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:29,000
I like the fresh air

769
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:31,000
and looking at the poor people in the infield.

770
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:48,200
I like the

771
00:40:48,200 --> 00:40:53,100
I don't even have to

772
00:40:53,100 --> 00:40:58,000
ask which one you like most.

773
00:40:58,000 --> 00:40:59,000
The Simpsons.

774
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:01,000
The Simpsons.

775
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:03,000
How many like The Simpsons most?

776
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:07,000
How many Shakespeare?

777
00:41:10,000 --> 00:41:12,000
What about Fear Factor?

778
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:14,000
How many preferred Fear Factor?

779
00:41:16,000 --> 00:41:17,000
Really?

780
00:41:21,000 --> 00:41:26,000
People overwhelmingly like The Simpsons better

781
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:29,000
than Shakespeare.

782
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:31,000
All right, now let's take

783
00:41:31,000 --> 00:41:33,000
the other part of the poll.

784
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:39,000
Which is the highest experience or pleasure?

785
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:43,000
How many say Shakespeare?

786
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:51,000
How many say Fear Factor?

787
00:41:51,000 --> 00:41:55,000
No, you can't be serious.

788
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:57,000
Really?

789
00:41:57,000 --> 00:41:59,000
What?

790
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:01,000
All right, go ahead.

791
00:42:01,000 --> 00:42:02,000
You can say it.

792
00:42:02,000 --> 00:42:04,000
I found that one the most entertaining.

793
00:42:04,000 --> 00:42:07,000
I know, but which do you think was the worthiest,

794
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:08,000
the noblest experience?

795
00:42:08,000 --> 00:42:10,000
I know you found it the most entertaining.

796
00:42:10,000 --> 00:42:13,000
If something is good just because it is pleasurable,

797
00:42:13,000 --> 00:42:15,000
what does it matter whether you

798
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:17,000
have sort of an abstract idea

799
00:42:17,000 --> 00:42:18,500
of whether it is good

800
00:42:18,500 --> 00:42:20,000
by someone else's sense or not?

801
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:21,500
All right, so you come

802
00:42:21,500 --> 00:42:23,000
down in the straight Benthamite side.

803
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:24,000
Who's the best?

804
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:26,000
Who's to judge?

805
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:28,000
And why should we judge?

806
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:30,000
Apart from just registering

807
00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:32,000
and aggregating de facto preferences.

808
00:42:32,000 --> 00:42:33,000
All right, that's fair enough.

809
00:42:33,000 --> 00:42:34,000
What's your name?

810
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:35,000
Nate.

811
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:36,000
Nate?

812
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:37,000
Okay, fair enough.

813
00:42:37,000 --> 00:42:40,000
All right, so how many think The Simpsons is actually,

814
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:44,000
apart from liking it, is actually the higher experience?

815
00:42:44,000 --> 00:42:47,000
Higher than Shakespeare.

816
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:49,000
All right, let's see the vote for Shakespeare again.

817
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:51,000
How many think Shakespeare is higher?

818
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:52,000
All right.

819
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:53,000
So why is it?

820
00:42:54,000 --> 00:42:56,000
Ideally, I'd like to hear from someone.

821
00:42:56,000 --> 00:43:01,000
Is there someone who thinks Shakespeare is highest,

822
00:43:01,000 --> 00:43:06,000
but who preferred watching The Simpsons?

823
00:43:06,000 --> 00:43:08,000
Yes.

824
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:10,000
Like, I guess just sitting watching The Simpsons,

825
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:11,000
it's entertaining because they make

826
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:12,000
jokes and they make us laugh,

827
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:14,000
but like someone has to tell

